  (150, 933.110, 934.389, 'They had to have him.'),
  (151, 935.990, 939.429, 'So for us, we couldn''t stay anymore.'),
  (152, 940.565, 947.445, 'So we did the same thing. We also took the train to the Netherlands.'),
  (153, 948.325, 953.360, 'The train ticket was for Berlin to Antwerp.'),
  (154, 954.879, 967.475, 'At the border at Oldenzaal, our brother who already lived here came to meet us.'),
  (155, 968.195, 974.595, 'And he said, ''You get off here,'' and we took another train to The Hague.'),
  (156, 974.835, 976.514, 'Then we came to The Hague.'),
  (157, 976.514, 977.955, 'What could you take with you?'),
  (158, 978.115, 978.615, 'Nothing.'),
  (159, 981.250, 983.889, 'Maybe a suitcase or something?'),
  (160, 984.290, 985.810, 'No, probably.'),
  (161, 986.050, 995.115, 'Just my father, my mother, and me. The three of us.'),
  (162, 995.995, 998.634, 'My sister was already married by then.'),
  (163, 1000.475, 1015.680, 'She also lived in Berlin. She was married with Warhaftig. And they came to the Netherlands a few months later as well.'),
  (164, 1016.160, 1020.639, 'The house you lived in, did it just stay there?'),
  (165, 1020.894, 1022.014, 'Yes, that''s how it was.'),
  (166, 1022.014, 1027.775, 'We lived on the second floor on that street.'),
  (167, 1027.775, 1029.855, 'And here, we got a new house.'),
  (168, 1029.934, 1031.694, 'But furniture and those kinds of things?'),
  (169, 1032.734, 1033.694, 'Just left behind.'),
  (170, 1033.694, 1034.194, 'Just left behind.'),
  (171, 1038.539, 1046.859, 'I do know that at first, the first address we had here, where we got a home.'),
  (172, 1047.420, 1049.660, 'Housing was very cheap here at the time.'),
  (173, 1050.394, 1063.355, 'On Schelderstraat. Later we lived on Radiolaan. Then everything was gone.'),
  (174, 1064.220, 1066.859, 'So you were taken in by your brother?'),
  (175, 1066.859, 1068.539, 'We were taken in by my brother.'),
  (176, 1068.539, 1070.139, 'And he took you to Amsterdam?'),
  (177, 1070.380, 1072.779, 'No, he also lived here in The Hague.'),
  (178, 1073.500, 1075.019, 'Yes, you came to The Hague?'),
  (179, 1075.019, 1076.299, 'Yes, came to The Hague.'),
  (180, 1076.299, 1083.424, 'My brother came to his brother-in-law who had a clothing business, women''s clothing.'),
  (181, 1086.945, 1089.904, 'I worked there for a while.'),
  (182, 1090.705, 1095.839, 'First I tried something else because I already wanted to go to Palestine then.'),
  (183, 1097.039, 1105.200, 'That didn''t work. It was very difficult because you couldn''t get a certificate, and then you had to go to hachshara.'),
  (184, 1105.440, 1107.680, 'It was all very complicated.'),
  (185, 1110.174, 1116.095, 'In the meantime, my mother had died young, 54 years old.'),
  (186, 1117.455, 1126.309, 'I was still working with my brother, in the business.'),
  (187, 1126.309, 1127.829, 'How did that work?'),
  (188, 1127.829, 1134.869, 'I mean, you come from Berlin, you come to The Hague, you get a house, there''s nothing in it, you have nothing.'),
  (189, 1134.869, 1137.190, 'How does'),
  (190, 1137.190, 1138.470, 'that work? You have to sleep?'),
  (191, 1139.750, 1148.515, 'It was fine. Well, that was fine, you could get that. Buy a few beds, a couple tables and chairs. How much do you need?'),
  (192, 1148.595, 1149.875, 'No, you don''t need much.'),
  (193, 1149.875, 1157.429, 'I don''t know if they had anything sent from Berlin. I can hardly imagine it.'),
  (194, 1158.309, 1160.549, 'I can''t remember. I don''t know anymore.'),
  (195, 1160.710, 1161.210, 'But'),
  (196, 1162.069, 1164.230, 'you could get work pretty easily, at least?'),
  (197, 1164.230, 1166.150, 'Well, not easily at all to get work.'),
  (198, 1166.150, 1166.950, 'I worked with my brother, yes.'),
  (199, 1166.950, 1167.910, 'With my brother, yes.'),
  (200, 1171.975, 1174.600, 'I did anything and everything, helping with machines.'),
  (201, 1175.600, 1186.600, 'Textiles, clothing. I believe mainly women''s clothing.'),
  (202, 1186.600, 1196.200, 'And at the end of ''37 I got engaged, beginning of ''38 maybe, end of ''37.'),
  (203, 1200.200, 1210.769, 'But I didn''t have, the salary you got was so minimal. Then you can''t start a family.'),
  (204, 1210.769, 1213.169, 'So I was still waiting.'),
  (205, 1213.809, 1215.329, 'Where did you meet your wife?'),
  (206, 1216.609, 1220.049, 'Here in Scheveningen. They had always lived in Scheveningen.'),
  (207, 1221.025, 1223.345, 'And they were members of the Jewish community and you saw them around?'),
  (208, 1223.345, 1224.144, 'Yes.'),
  (209, 1224.384, 1229.825, 'The Jewish community, at my parents-in-law was a bakery and a shop, also on Gentsestraat.'),
  (210, 1229.825, 1232.224, 'The bakery was on IJmuiderstraat.'),
  (211, 1234.460, 1236.619, 'And that''s how I met my wife.'),
  (212, 1240.220, 1253.634, 'Meanwhile it was all a tense time. You know what all happened: ''37, ''38 then with the Anschluss, and so on and so on.'),
  (213, 1257.550, 1258.050, 'Kristalnacht'),
  (214, 1274.865, 1281.024, 'When it was almost ''39, it became very difficult.'),
  (215, 1282.144, 1286.544, 'The threat of war was very great. At least, that''s what I saw.'),
  (216, 1288.329, 1291.289, 'My parents-in-law, for example, didn''t see that at all.'),
  (217, 1291.289, 1292.809, 'Not your father either?'),
  (218, 1292.809, 1294.649, 'My father did.'),
  (219, 1296.169, 1306.200, 'I forgot to tell that. What we experienced in Berlin and those things, until we fled.'),
  (220, 1306.200, 1319.200, 'On Shabbat we went from shul home, we walked through narrow alleys, with my brother-in-law and my father and me. I was still a little boy, I was 15 years old.'),
  (221, 1320.109, 1326.429, 'And then suddenly SA men came toward us.'),
  (222, 1327.630, 1329.149, 'My father had a beard.'),
  (223, 1333.951, 1337.233, 'It is a very narrow street, as wide as that room'),
  (224, 1341.200, 1346.640, 'He shouted at my father ''You Jew-swine, aren''t you gone from here yet?!'''),
  (225, 1346.640, 1360.000, 'On a Sunday, I went to get something. Suddenly I saw on the street SA men who wore those white armbands with ''Hilfspolizei'' written on them.'),
  (226, 1360.000, 1365.676, 'You know what those are? SA men, the brownshirts.'),
  (227, 1366.000, 1390.160, 'It was apparently a Jewish neighborhood, and they came from synagogue, or in any case, they encountered those people and beat them with rubber clubs, just like that on the street. Those are things that always stay with you.'),
  (228, 1391.215, 1413.819, 'Well, then in ''33. My brother, yes, in ''35 my second brother Marcus Mordecai went. He went with his wife and child, with his son and they got a certificate.'),
  (229, 1415.259, 1423.034, 'So it was also very interesting, because they got it. He had made a connection with someone from the English embassy.'),
  (230, 1423.515, 1430.154, 'That''s how he got a certificate, because from the Jewish Agency it was very difficult to get the certificate.'),
  (231, 1433.210, 1436.970, 'He went to Palestine, lived there a whole time.'),
  (232, 1438.170, 1449.484, 'And in 1939, the war broke out. September, September 1st, 1939, right.'),
  (233, 1452.285, 1464.390, 'That brother-in-law of my brother who lived here, my oldest brother''s brother-in-law, he already went - he was a Dutchman - he already went with his whole family to Chile.'),
  (234, 1465.509, 1470.444, 'Also my brother, he got his visa.'),
  (235, 1471.325, 1485.599, 'And he left December 4th, ''39. December 4th, ''39 to Chile by boat via Trieste, Italy.'),
  (236, 1491.960, 1492.460, 'Yes.'),
  (237, 1493.359, 1494.880, 'Yes, well how does it go with me?'),
  (238, 1497.305, 1514.890, 'Then it was, so they had a business and that was taken over by a firm in Rotterdam, Mr. Hartog, he took over that business and appointed me as manager in The Hague.'),
  (239, 1514.890, 1521.049, 'He had a business in Rotterdam. Still a very large business with or over 100 workers.'),
  (240, 1525.465, 1527.384, 'Here it was a smaller company.'),
  (241, 1527.384, 1530.105, 'Was that Mr. Hartog also in textiles?'),
  (242, 1530.105, 1534.744, 'Yes, yes, he had a large clothing factory in Rotterdam.'),
  (243, 1534.744, 1536.585, 'A well-known firm, Hartog.'),
  (244, 1538.799, 1541.519, 'A great man.'),
  (245, 1541.519, 1548.960, 'And I became manager and I got, a decent salary for then.'),
  (246, 1550.160, 1552.335, 'So I could marry.'),
  (247, 1554.494, 1567.190, 'And I stayed living in the building where that business was, because that was, it was actually property of my brother''s brother-in-law.'),
  (248, 1568.470, 1570.390, 'And rent had to be paid there.'),
  (249, 1576.105, 1579.144, 'That was early 1940.'),
  (250, 1580.585, 1587.464, 'The chuppah, do you know what that is? I had the chuppah, December 31st, ''39, the last day of the year.'),
  (251, 1588.950, 1590.549, 'The last day of the year.'),
  (252, 1594.630, 1598.950, 'Well, then I was the manager there in that building.'),
  (253, 1604.224, 1610.944, 'Early March ''40.'),
  (254, 1612.384, 1615.184, 'Hartog said, ''Listen, this isn''t going to work, you just have to move that whole business to Rotterdam.'''),
  (255, 1615.184, 1617.345, 'You have to move the whole business to Rotterdam.'''),
  (256, 1619.950, 1622.829, 'Well, that''s not so easy for me.'),
  (257, 1627.230, 1632.075, 'And then I go to my parents-in-law, with my father, my sister, what should I do?'),
  (258, 1634.154, 1640.075, 'My parents-in-law said: try, maybe take over a part of that business.'),
  (259, 1640.555, 1662.804, 'And then, Mr. Hartog says to me: ''Jonas, you may have this and that of the machines, you may use all that. And then we''ll see.'),
  (260, 1663.445, 1669.285, 'but I have to have this and this amount, there''s no discussing that, you can pay it or you can''t pay it.'''),
  (261, 1670.164, 1675.390, '"I don''t know. I''ll consult with my parents-in-law, it wasn''t a large amount."'),
  (262, 1676.350, 1681.309, 'I said, "Yes, I want to do it, but I need material to work with."'),
  (263, 1681.309, 1686.934, 'He said: ''Look, listen, you''re first going to see if you can sell.'),
  (264, 1686.934, 1691.734, 'Just make a few models. If you can sell, then we''ll look further.'''),
  (265, 1695.174, 1698.615, 'I got an order from Haas Fashion.'),
  (266, 1700.470, 1704.069, 'Have you ever heard of that? That it was the corner of the Spui. I believe 2 or 300 dresses.'),
  (267, 1705.269, 1723.924, 'He said, ''Look, the goods that are lying here, you can use those, but otherwise I''ll send you goods from Rotterdam, and after three months you just pay me back.''')) v(seq, s, e, x)
where t.slug = 'tape-1';

-- Tape 2: captions (2).sbv, 232 cues
insert into entities (type, slug, name, attrs) values ('tape', 'tape-2', 'Tape 2', '{"number": 2, "youtube_id": null, "duration_sec": 1765.48}') on conflict (slug) do nothing;
delete from cues where lang = 'en' and tape_id = (select id from entities where slug = 'tape-2');
insert into cues (tape_id, lang, seq, start_sec, end_sec, text)
select t.id, 'en', v.seq, v.s, v.e, v.x from entities t, (values
  (1, 0.640, 2.399, 'That Mr. Hartog could take over.'),
  (2, 3.120, 4.799, 'A large order from that Hague fashion house.'),
  (3, 4.799, 17.964, 'Yes, you can find it in the Chamber of Commerce. It''s all written precisely that I took over a business from Mr. Hartog, and that was in April.'),
  (4, 25.644, 29.324, 'May 10th was a Friday morning.'),
  (5, 32.479, 39.199, 'We were in bed and it was a beautiful day.'),
  (6, 40.479, 43.519, 'And I''m walking and I hear airplane engines.'),
  (7, 45.155, 49.315, 'I walk to the window and I see an airplane.'),
  (8, 51.715, 55.875, 'I told my wife ''Do (Dora), it''s war''.'),
  (9, 59.810, 62.289, 'She says, ''Jonas, you''re crazy, go to bed.'''),
  (10, 63.730, 65.489, 'It was 4 o''clock in the morning.'),
  (11, 68.209, 86.239, 'Well, a few minutes later more came and I suddenly saw that cross - you know, the German one - on the airplane. Well, it''s over. This is it. It''s war.'),
  (12, 87.359, 93.200, 'And we had a distribution radio then. Do you know what that is?'),
  (13, 93.920, 97.039, 'There was no one then, because we had this.'),
  (14, 97.905, 101.584, 'This usually only came on at 7 o''clock.'),
  (15, 103.185, 107.025, 'I turned on the radio and the radio worked.'),
  (16, 107.665, 118.780, 'And then you heard, ''Here is air service, here is air service''. They were announcing that enemy aircraft are coming.'),
  (17, 120.140, 124.299, 'In the area of The Hague, parachutists have come down.'),
  (18, 128.794, 136.155, 'So into the street. I don''t know what else we did. No one came to the business.'),
  (19, 136.155, 141.594, 'We had that business. I had about 6 or 7 girls working.'),
  (20, 144.360, 145.400, 'Were you scared?'),
  (21, 146.360, 147.560, 'Yes, I was scared.'),
  (22, 148.439, 153.879, 'Something else I''m forgetting.'),
  (23, 154.599, 160.474, 'Actually, I got civilly married on May 10th, 1939.'),
  (24, 162.715, 165.995, 'The reason was, I wanted to go to America.'),
  (25, 167.594, 172.009, 'And since I was born in the Netherlands, it was the Dutch quota.'),
  (26, 174.714, 186.718, 'And my wife was born in the Sudetenland which belonged then... when she was born, I don''t know if that belonged to Germany.'),
  (27, 189.675, 191.395, 'In any case, later it was Czechoslovakia.'),
  (28, 194.428, 198.995, 'So she couldn''t get in. The quota was closed.'),
  (29, 200.590, 209.150, 'But if we got married, then... That''s why we got married civilly on May 10th, 1939.'),
  (30, 209.150, 210.270, 'But that didn''t work out?'),
  (31, 210.750, 211.950, 'It didn''t work out, no.'),
  (32, 213.514, 214.875, 'We needed money.'),
  (33, 217.354, 229.270, 'Well, ''40. We went to my parents to my parents-in-law we went, to Scheveningen.'),
  (34, 231.030, 236.870, 'We stayed there until May 14th.'),
  (35, 240.775, 257.189, 'We saw the pier we were standing there. Then you suddenly saw after 2 hours, between 2 and 3 o''clock, you already saw the smoke clouds from Rotterdam'),
  (36, 257.189, 259.270, 'Because Rotterdam was bombed.'),
  (37, 259.350, 262.870, 'Then Holland capitulated.'),
  (38, 263.189, 267.110, 'It was over at 4 o''clock, but the bombardment was at 2 o''clock.'),
  (39, 272.805, 278.084, 'A few days later, we went back home.'),
  (40, 278.324, 280.324, 'We just went back to work.'),
  (41, 280.404, 281.524, 'The girls came.'),
  (42, 282.470, 288.629, 'I had a German girl - she was the cutter, a German girl.'),
  (43, 295.990, 304.514, 'We worked, and then the Jewish decrees came.'),
  (44, 304.514, 309.235, 'No, it was 1942, 1941.'),
  (45, 313.599, 316.079, '1940 was still fairly quiet.'),
  (46, 316.800, 320.959, 'In 1941, you had to register your businesses.'),
  (47, 323.625, 336.985, 'You had to get your... the card what''s it called the identity card.'),
  (48, 341.389, 355.185, 'The personal identification. First came the personal identification, I can remember. It was right near us. Next to where we lived there was a school, and there you could pick up the personal identification'),
  (49, 357.904, 365.745, 'I was sitting with a man who was from the Netherlands, from the civil registry.'),
  (50, 366.379, 370.060, 'So everyone got that. Everyone got a personal identification.'),
  (51, 371.259, 377.259, 'I was sitting with that man, and then with the personal identification, you got a ''J'' on it.'),
  (52, 382.035, 387.555, 'I was alone, so I only had one for my wife and for me.'),
  (53, 387.795, 396.754, 'I said to that official, what I would never forget, I said... because he had all these stamps. There were stamps on it.'),
  (54, 397.470, 409.550, 'Yes, then that one still had to... it was already on the card, because they had to register, I think already in 1940. Jewish businesses had to be registered.'),
  (55, 411.314, 417.395, 'Then I said to that man, ''Listen, you don''t need to put that on there, just leave it off.'''),
  (56, 418.995, 420.435, 'He looked at me as if I''m crazy.'),
  (57, 423.529, 425.690, 'Well, that was 1941.'),
  (58, 425.930, 437.529, 'During ''41 it was. Then came the raids. In the winter, the February strike.'),
  (59, 441.664, 449.504, 'End of ''41, several people fled from The Hague.'),
  (60, 450.305, 455.440, 'Several left alone without their families.'),
  (61, 456.319, 466.400, 'I had acquaintances, a friend from Amsterdam, Bernard de Leeuw, and he said, ''Boys, maybe we should leave too.'''),
  (62, 469.024, 470.144, 'I''m not leaving.'),
  (63, 470.704, 476.064, 'And then in 1941, my son was born on July 15th.'),
  (64, 481.024, 481.904, 'I''m not leaving.'),
  (65, 485.839, 505.875, 'Then came ''42. It all went very quickly. The 24th - that was June 24th, I think, on a Friday.'),
  (66, 511.479, 514.120, 'I didn''t get an administrator until then.'),
  (67, 514.200, 516.600, 'Most businesses already had an administrator.'),
  (68, 517.080, 518.279, 'I didn''t have an administrator.'),
  (69, 522.805, 526.644, 'I got a phone call from the Wirtschaftsprüfstelle.'),
  (70, 526.644, 527.764, 'Do you know what that was?'),
  (71, 527.845, 532.884, 'It was an agency, a German agency, that was on the Korte Vijverberg here in The Hague.'),
  (72, 533.685, 545.889, 'And they sent a form every month during the course of ''41, ''42 to fill in whether we could do any work for the Wehrmacht.'),
  (73, 546.450, 550.529, 'And I kept writing back.'),
  (74, 552.235, 556.794, 'I have too little capacity, so I can''t do that.'),
  (75, 558.154, 564.475, 'On that infamous Friday, that man calls. He says, ''Jonas Stemmer, ladies'' clothing''.'),
  (76, 566.519, 570.759, 'I keep getting those forms back. Why is that?'),
  (77, 572.679, 576.759, 'He spoke in German, so I said, ''I don''t understand German. What do you mean?'''),
  (78, 579.674, 581.674, 'That I always send it back.'),
  (79, 581.674, 586.714, 'Yes, I have a small business, so the work I have is for those customers.'),
  (80, 589.834, 591.355, 'Then he said, ''Is it an Aryan business?'''),
  (81, 593.100, 594.059, 'Do you know what I mean?'),
  (82, 596.523, 600.539, 'No. That was Friday.'),
  (83, 603.259, 607.355, 'We made contact with various people because I wanted to leave.'),
  (84, 608.394, 612.394, 'Now is the time to try.'),
  (85, 612.394, 621.035, 'But yes, I had family here. My in-laws with 3 daughters, 2 grandchildren, twins of 3 years old.'),
  (86, 621.839, 627.679, 'I have my sister with 4 children, my father with my stepmother.'),
  (87, 629.120, 630.320, 'What should I do?'),
  (88, 630.320, 638.785, 'I want to leave because they called me up for the work camps, so-called work camps.'),
  (89, 642.225, 652.559, 'I made contact with a woman in the city, on Rembrandtstraat, who would bring us to Belgium.'),
  (90, 654.319, 669.054, 'And we left Wednesday morning, June 24th, 1942. It was a very warm day. A coat with some clothes and a bag.'),
  (91, 669.694, 671.214, 'A backpack. That was all.'),
  (92, 672.574, 677.134, 'We said we''re going with the star. We had to wear a star then.'),
  (93, 678.014, 679.054, 'It was all there then.'),
  (94, 680.959, 683.200, 'Only 2 months, but very short.'),
  (95, 686.639, 693.039, 'We didn''t live far from the station, the Holland Spoor station on the Nieuwe Schoolstraat.'),
  (96, 695.435, 706.955, 'And that woman had already bought the tickets, train tickets. And she took the child by the window because he was already 10 months old.'),
  (97, 709.769, 715.289, 'We walked behind each other, and on the way to the station, we took off our coats.'),
  (98, 715.769, 719.529, 'Because you couldn''t get on the train with the star.'),
  (99, 724.149, 728.887, 'And then we arrived in Bergen op Zoom.'),
  (100, 728.887, 736.185, 'Wht did you have in the bag?'),
  (101, 736.185, 738.185, 'Talit and Tefilin.'),
  (102, 741.240, 742.839, 'Well, that''s good.'),
  (103, 743.480, 747.799, 'So we arrived at the border.'),
  (104, 749.945, 751.465, 'First we got off.'),
  (105, 752.584, 760.664, 'Yes, because I had sent a baby carriage ahead to the station, and we picked it up there.'),
  (106, 760.904, 780.705, 'And when we wanted to go, that woman had someone else to bring us over the border. That man says, ''Sorry, it''s not happening tonight. It''s not going through. The border is too dangerous.'''),
  (107, 781.504, 790.670, 'I said, ''Yes, I promised my in-laws and my father that I would send them a telegram from Antwerp that everything is okay.'''),
  (108, 792.350, 796.029, 'I said, ''We''ll go to the farm.'''),
  (109, 796.350, 801.470, 'I said to the farmer, ''Listen, you''re going to the village and you''re sending the telegram''.'),
  (110, 804.914, 811.714, 'My wife, it was the first time in her life she was away from her parents. She wanted to go back.'),
  (111, 813.680, 815.053, 'She started crying.'),
  (112, 817.139, 820.820, 'I said, ''There is no going back''.'),
  (113, 821.459, 822.579, 'We''re going through.'),
  (114, 822.899, 840.715, 'Yes, the next day, I have way too much to tell. The next day a little girl came who brought us to a certain place in the border area. There we had to wait for someone else who would pick us up.'),
  (115, 841.514, 842.394, 'And he didn''t come.'),
  (116, 843.259, 844.940, 'And the girl started crying.'),
  (117, 845.820, 852.220, 'I said, ''You go home. We''ll stay here. We''ll wait for that man.'''),
  (118, 852.299, 856.634, 'And that man came. He brought us over the border.'),
  (119, 857.274, 859.035, 'Border... you don''t really see it like that.'),
  (120, 859.754, 866.475, 'And we arrived at a small station, and there was a small train to Antwerp.'),
  (121, 868.090, 871.529, 'We left the baby carriage there.'),
  (122, 874.889, 881.450, 'We arrive in Antwerp, and I take out the carriage and put the child in it.'),
  (123, 883.735, 887.334, 'And I lost the train tickets.'),
  (124, 889.815, 891.174, 'Do you know Antwerp?'),
  (125, 891.174, 893.414, 'Then it goes down like this, the stairs going down.'),
  (126, 897.610, 901.769, 'Then you had to go through control. You had to hand in tickets.'),
  (127, 903.929, 905.210, 'I didn''t have them.'),
  (128, 906.089, 910.595, 'And a train came, I think from Brussels, with many people.'),
  (129, 912.274, 925.169, 'I went through there, and that guy, ticket, le billet, le billet in French. I roll with that little carriage down, the stairs down, and disappeared.'),
  (130, 925.809, 934.929, 'My wife had distant family or something she knew in Antwerp in the Jewish neighborhood.'),
  (131, 937.225, 944.264, 'Yes, we came here without a star.'),
  (132, 946.424, 949.544, '''Yes, we just escaped from there.'''),
  (133, 950.480, 956.240, '''Are you completely crazy? Walking around without stars? Do you know how dangerous that is here?'''),
  (134, 957.200, 960.559, 'Well, they quickly went to buy stars for us.'),
  (135, 960.639, 964.080, 'Stars in Belgium are different from in the Netherlands.'),
  (136, 964.080, 968.254, 'In the Netherlands it said ''Jood'' and in Belgium it only said ''J''.'),
  (137, 970.000, 980.360, 'Jood in Dutch and Juif in French. Also J.'),
  (138, 985.490, 990.309, 'We stayed there about a week, I think. A short week.'),
  (139, 991.509, 994.470, 'Then we went to my wife''s cousin, in Brussels.'),
  (140, 997.684, 1003.605, 'Our goal was to get to so-called ''unoccupied France.'''),
  (141, 1003.605, 1005.486, 'Vichy France'),
  (142, 1005.486, 1014.348, 'We thought we might survive the war there.'),
  (143, 1017.278, 1020.280, 'We got to Brussels.'),
  (144, 1020.280, 1023.850, 'We found smugglers if you know what that is.'),
  (145, 1024.775, 1026.454, 'I won''t mention the name.'),
  (146, 1030.615, 1044.079, 'There were 14 of us: 2 smugglers and 12 people, including 2 women - my wife and another woman - and our child.'),
  (147, 1045.039, 1056.355, 'And we left Brussels, not to Paris, because it was too dangerous. We had no papers with us at all.'),
  (148, 1056.355, 1056.914, 'None.'),
  (149, 1058.914, 1067.360, 'Only that personal identification and that''s no good of course.'),
  (150, 1070.360, 1074.030, 'We went to south east Belgium.'),
  (151, 1074.030, 1075.695, 'With the train?'),
  (152, 1075.695, 1077.695, 'With the train.'),
  (153, 1080.732, 1084.335, 'There''s a border town, Mézières Charleville.'),
  (154, 1084.994, 1097.589, 'That''s one, but the twelve of us walked over the mountains to that place.'),
  (155, 1097.590, 1109.154, 'And my wife with the child went by train that bit with a woman, Belgian or French, I don''t know exactly.'),
  (156, 1110.035, 1113.714, 'We then walked, I don''t know how many hours, all night.'),
  (157, 1114.355, 1120.929, 'She went by train, and that was also dangerous, of course, because you went with that woman.'),
  (158, 1122.049, 1128.609, 'So we came to Mézières Charleville, then we went to sleep in a hotel for one night.'),
  (159, 1128.929, 1140.144, 'The next night we went by train. If you have Brussels here, then it''s southeast. Then we went back towards Paris.'),
  (160, 1143.025, 1158.170, 'We had to transfer. We stopped in Reims and arrived in Paris on Friday afternoon.'),
  (161, 1160.325, 1165.764, 'What we didn''t know was that it was extremely dangerous. Paris was a forbidden zone.'),
  (162, 1165.845, 1170.325, 'Besides being occupied territory, it was completely forbidden.'),
  (163, 1170.484, 1172.884, 'So if you got caught on the train there.'),
  (164, 1176.349, 1187.789, 'Sunday morning... yes, those 2 smugglers brought us to a small hotel, I don''t know, in the center of Paris.'),
  (165, 1188.795, 1192.234, 'And they slept somewhere else.'),
  (166, 1194.795, 1198.474, 'By the way, there were German soldiers in that hotel too.'),
  (167, 1201.835, 1210.399, 'Sunday morning at 7 o''clock, I look out the window, second or third floor, I don''t know exactly.'),
  (168, 1210.640, 1220.944, 'There were police on all 4 corners, and everyone walking on the street was stopped. They had to show their identity card.'),
  (169, 1222.545, 1225.424, 'Not so good. How do you get out?'),
  (170, 1228.144, 1239.900, 'At 8 o''clock, a police car came, police cars, and those 4 men on the 4 corners got in and left.'),
  (171, 1241.740, 1246.095, '5 minutes later, those 2 smugglers came. ''We''re going.'''),
  (172, 1249.055, 1261.079, 'Not far, I don''t remember how far it was, maybe 100 100 or 200 meters or so to the metro. We went to Gare d''Austerlitz.'),
  (173, 1263.160, 1265.159, 'Gare d''Austerlitz. It goes to the south.'),
  (174, 1267.880, 1273.044, 'We arrived at Gare d''Austerlitz. Very few people.'),
  (175, 1274.404, 1279.444, 'Sunday morning, 9 o''clock, the train left.'),
  (176, 1279.684, 1283.524, 'We went to Tours, middle of France.'),
  (177, 1285.220, 1295.779, 'On that Sunday was the biggest roundup in Paris, July 1942.'),
  (178, 1297.380, 1300.660, '30 or 40,000 people were arrested then.'),
  (179, 1302.714, 1313.434, 'In Tours, we also came to a small villa or something, all of us together.'),
  (180, 1316.400, 1331.724, 'That was about, I think, 50 or 60 kilometers from the line of demarcation, the border between occupied and unoccupied France.'),
  (181, 1335.565, 1340.605, 'We were brought there in a small truck.'),
  (182, 1343.809, 1352.690, 'Then we ended up in a small village, or hamlet, where you''d only see German soldiers.'),
  (183, 1353.809, 1364.214, 'One of those smugglers brought us to the café, those 2 men, ''Come on in.'),
  (184, 1365.494, 1377.409, 'And they talked with young men or I don''t know and they said about bringing us over the border.'),
  (185, 1378.049, 1384.929, 'Apparently that didn''t work. They were drunk. In any case, we were mainly glad we could leave.'),
  (186, 1385.184, 1388.704, 'But we still had to pay, I believe, everyone 1000 francs.'),
  (187, 1388.785, 1390.865, 'Because we were sitting there in the café.'),
  (188, 1391.744, 1396.944, 'So that was July.'),
  (189, 1398.384, 1402.480, 'We come out of the café, in the hamlet.'),
  (190, 1403.200, 1409.679, 'And there are 14 people with a bag dressed as city people, not farmers.'),
  (191, 1412.640, 1414.480, 'And they don''t know what we should do.'),
  (192, 1414.480, 1416.000, 'Those 2 men don''t either.'),
  (193, 1416.575, 1418.014, 'What should we do now?'),
  (194, 1421.055, 1445.845, 'Then a couple comes towards us with a baby carriage. And one of the people who were with us, there were two brothers, twins. What were their names again, I''ve forgotten? They spoke good French. They came from Brussels, most were from Belgium anyway.'),
  (195, 1446.805, 1447.444, 'All of them.'),
  (196, 1449.605, 1455.445, 'And he went to that man and that woman. And he said, ''Listen, do you know where we can sleep?'''),
  (197, 1457.339, 1459.659, 'He was a Belgian farmer.'),
  (198, 1461.579, 1467.420, 'And he said, ''We are refugees from Belgium, not Jewish refugees but refugees nonetheless.'''),
  (199, 1470.194, 1474.114, '''Come to my farm.'''),
  (200, 1476.994, 1484.849, 'We slept there. My wife and the child and that other woman got to sleep inside.'),
  (201, 1485.009, 1487.089, 'We slept in a haystack.'),
  (202, 1497.625, 1502.904, 'We had to eat something. Bread, milk, eggs, I don''t know what, butter.'),
  (203, 1507.625, 1512.499, 'Then we said, ''Listen, we want to cross the border.'''),
  (204, 1515.700, 1517.379, '''Find someone for us.'''),
  (205, 1517.619, 1535.034, 'He said, ''Tomorrow evening at 10, 10:30, you have to go to that spot in the forest. Then you have to continue with that man.'''),
  (206, 1536.315, 1542.750, 'The whole day we stayed with him on the farm and then he says, ''You have to go then.'''),
  (207, 1546.990, 1549.789, 'Then he said, ''Yes, if you come with us,'' because he was a bit scared.'),
  (208, 1549.869, 1553.964, 'Because there was, I think, actually a German camp.'),
  (209, 1556.045, 1561.540, 'He went with us. We came to the forest.'),
  (210, 1561.805, 1566.045, 'It was dark, a bit. It was summer.'),
  (211, 1570.669, 1580.509, 'Those 2 smugglers were again the people who did the deal. They negotiated again with that young man. They had to pay for us.'),
  (212, 1588.525, 1598.380, 'We started walking, until 4 o''clock in the morning. From 10:30.'),
  (213, 1598.940, 1603.579, 'Each time someone else carried the child. He wasn''t allowed to cry.'),
  (214, 1607.535, 1614.654, 'At 4 in the morning, that boy says... he also had signs of where to walk, how to walk.'),
  (215, 1616.174, 1618.494, 'He says, ''I''m lost.'''),
  (216, 1621.295, 1622.335, 'It was through the forest.'),
  (217, 1622.910, 1631.309, 'No road, through the forest, fields, no path.'),
  (218, 1636.684, 1638.924, 'I thought, I need to see where I am.'),
  (219, 1640.285, 1644.684, 'Before that it was a bit like you couldn''t see so well.'),
  (220, 1648.365, 1649.244, 'He came back.'),
  (221, 1652.029, 1655.069, 'We walked until 6 o''clock in the morning.'),
  (222, 1657.390, 1663.710, 'Along the way we encountered five more refugees who said, ''Just keep walking.'''),
  (223, 1664.505, 1672.744, 'We came to a large farm. There we ate and drank milk and gave butter to the child.'),
  (224, 1673.464, 1681.690, 'And that smuggler, the agreement was that he would bring us from Brussels to Lyon.'),
  (225, 1684.169, 1685.769, 'He left us in the lurch.'),
  (226, 1687.210, 1696.424, 'I gave him another watch. Then we had to pay part, and pay the rest when we''re in Lyon.'),
  (227, 1696.505, 1703.970, 'And then he wanted a note from me that I... that we... that he brought us safely to Lyon.'),
  (228, 1703.970, 1706.210, '''I''m not giving that. I''m not in Lyon yet.'''),
  (229, 1708.529, 1715.490, '''A car will come that will bring you to the station, and from there you continue.'''),
  (230, 1719.255, 1720.934, 'Not a car. A horse cart.'),
  (231, 1722.295, 1749.585, 'We sat down and went with that little horse cart and arrived at that little station. And we got on that train, where we were twelve yes, it was a little train with 2 passenger cars.'),
  (232, 1751.184, 1760.480, 'And 5 of us went in the front part, and the others went in the second part, the second car.')) v(seq, s, e, x)
where t.slug = 'tape-2';

-- Tape 3: captions (3).sbv, 225 cues
insert into entities (type, slug, name, attrs) values ('tape', 'tape-3', 'Tape 3', '{"number": 3, "youtube_id": null, "duration_sec": 1809.38}') on conflict (slug) do nothing;
delete from cues where lang = 'en' and tape_id = (select id from entities where slug = 'tape-3');
insert into cues (tape_id, lang, seq, start_sec, end_sec, text)
select t.id, 'en', v.seq, v.s, v.e, v.x from entities t, (values
  (1, 0.719, 3.600, 'You got on after being brought there.'),
  (2, 3.600, 4.400, 'Yes, yes.'),
  (3, 4.400, 5.679, 'On the horse cart.'),
  (4, 6.960, 24.054, 'We got on the train and suddenly we saw, the train made a stop. We saw those 7 who got into the second car being pulled out by the gendarmes.'),
  (5, 26.454, 32.679, 'There were 5 of us: my wife, with my child, and 2 others.'),
  (6, 36.600, 38.119, 'It was something terrible.'),
  (7, 38.119, 40.600, 'We thought, ''Now it''s our turn.'''),
  (8, 40.600, 43.880, 'The train went on. We arrived in Limoges.'),
  (9, 49.684, 55.284, 'We arrived in Limoges at the station. A big station. Limoges is a big station.'),
  (10, 55.284, 57.759, 'Porcelain city.'),
  (11, 61.520, 63.200, 'And we wanted to go to Lyon.'),
  (12, 65.439, 81.064, 'Well, the man who was with us, one of the twins was there and another man. They went to get tickets for Lyon.'),
  (13, 86.659, 89.219, 'The train left at midnight.'),
  (14, 95.780, 99.140, 'You could still rent a little cushion.'),
  (15, 100.500, 106.995, 'The train arrives. It was packed, packed, packed full.'),
  (16, 108.275, 109.555, 'To our luck.'),
  (17, 110.674, 112.834, 'We come in with the child.'),
  (18, 114.915, 118.950, 'The train has corridors.'),
  (19, 121.590, 129.349, 'Well, I saw that a woman with a small child... someone stood up and gave that seat to my wife and child.'),
  (20, 129.430, 133.605, 'We all lay there in the corridors, on top of each other.'),
  (21, 133.764, 137.525, 'It was so full. Luckily, there could be no inspection.'),
  (22, 138.405, 143.844, 'We arrived in Lyon at 1 o''clock in the afternoon.'),
  (23, 145.629, 147.710, 'So we were on the train for 12 hours.'),
  (24, 148.750, 154.750, 'I still don''t know how we gave our child, what we gave our child.'),
  (25, 154.750, 155.950, 'But I don''t remember anymore.'),
  (26, 157.310, 169.694, 'We arrived in Lyon. There were no taxis. It was also a horse cart, what''s it called, a carriage.'),
  (27, 171.694, 179.879, 'We had an address. It was a sister of my sister-in-law, my brother''s wife who went to Chile.'),
  (28, 180.519, 188.120, 'They left Antwerp already in 1940. They stayed living in Lyon.'),
  (29, 190.424, 191.944, 'We went there.'),
  (30, 192.905, 194.264, 'We had the address.'),
  (31, 195.784, 200.424, 'Well, she told me, ''You have to see where you can sleep.'''),
  (32, 200.424, 221.985, 'So I go to restaurant Am Brun. You could find more people from Holland there. I left my wife there for a moment. I went there and I met a friend of mine, Berent Bekker, from The Hague.'),
  (33, 222.224, 228.108, 'I said, ''Listen, I need somewhere to sleep.'''),
  (34, 228.689, 237.729, 'Go to hotel St. Pothin, the street is also called St. Pothin. Maybe he has a place for you.'),
  (35, 237.729, 239.010, 'So I went there.'),
  (36, 239.409, 242.824, 'They said, ''I''m sorry, everything is full.'''),
  (37, 243.465, 260.139, '''You have to look anyway. I have a room. Those people are also from Holland. Those people are in the countryside now. They''ll only come back in a week.'''),
  (38, 260.220, 264.620, '''You can sleep that week. Maybe that''s what we''ll do.'''),
  (39, 265.980, 270.665, 'And so it was. We went there. We could sleep.'),
  (40, 271.625, 276.105, 'Those people came back. There was still no place.'),
  (41, 276.745, 284.985, 'Those people had 2 beds in it, and I had a baby carriage.'),
  (42, 286.080, 294.720, 'Those people were a couple with a girl, 7, 8 years old, I think.'),
  (43, 297.004, 301.004, 'We slept for a week in this room.'),
  (44, 301.884, 302.764, 'Together.'),
  (45, 305.644, 309.805, 'Unfortunately, they''ve all passed away. A daughter lives here in The Hague.'),
  (46, 309.805, 311.644, 'She was born here after the war.'),
  (47, 316.889, 334.084, 'That was the beginning when we arrived. Then the niece I told you about from Brussels also unfortunately came with her husband and son to Lyon.'),
  (48, 336.960, 341.120, 'And then the raids began.'),
  (49, 351.715, 368.689, 'Meanwhile I already had, yes, I already had the so-called passport, a pass, it was a document from the Office Néerlandais and Sally Noach'),
  (50, 370.129, 371.250, 'I was given that.'),
  (51, 371.810, 377.250, 'Also with many miracles that I was given that.'),
  (52, 380.024, 389.705, 'I met someone at the top of that office and he says, ''Yes, he knows me,'' because he had to testify that I''m from Holland, that I''m Dutch.'),
  (53, 390.264, 392.345, 'I got it.'),
  (54, 396.810, 409.665, 'We lived in a hotel, from there we had to leave St. Potin and we came to a hotel called Quai Jair. On the water.'),
  (55, 409.665, 410.785, 'Quai Jayr.'),
  (56, 413.264, 419.585, 'The niece also lived there, with her husband and her son.'),
  (57, 420.145, 434.000, 'At 1, 1:30. The National Police came. ''Open the door''.'),
  (58, 434.000, 445.367, '''Papers.'' First I didn''t open. The whole hotel was full of refugees. Screaming. Well, I opened the door.'),
  (59, 449.551, 453.459, 'I opened the door. I let them in.'),
  (60, 454.073, 464.828, '''Must come.'' I said, ''Now I can''t come, there''s a one-year-old child lying here, are you completely crazy?'''),
  (61, 465.285, 468.245, '''Yes, there''s nothing to be done about it.'''),
  (62, 468.245, 478.245, 'I started screaming and luckily because of that, several police came to my room.'),
  (63, 478.245, 479.044, '''What''s going on?'''),
  (64, 480.210, 485.410, 'So I, well, I''m not leaving, I have a small child, could I speak French, I couldn''t speak it anyway.'),
  (65, 487.170, 491.650, 'So some people could get up and run away.'),
  (66, 494.544, 500.944, 'We had to get up. We were brought to a very small police station.'),
  (67, 506.720, 513.279, 'It was at night. At 6 o''clock in the morning, it was day. The child had nothing, hadn''t drunk anything yet.'),
  (68, 513.679, 519.714, 'I said to that police, ''Listen, I need some milk for this child. I need something.'''),
  (69, 519.714, 521.475, '''Yes, can''t do it.'''),
  (70, 525.154, 528.595, 'Well, screaming, so I was allowed to go with an officer.'),
  (71, 529.315, 533.990, 'Across the way was a milk shop, and I got some milk there.'),
  (72, 535.029, 539.190, 'We went back to the station because my wife was there.'),
  (73, 544.470, 554.075, 'Around 3 o''clock, I think, 2 or 3, I went in a big police van.'),
  (74, 554.075, 564.129, 'We all had to get in there. I think about 20 people.'),
  (75, 568.049, 574.929, 'And we were brought to the Sûreté. I don''t know what it is. The main police headquarters.'),
  (76, 579.225, 591.300, 'We come in there to the courtyard. Full of people.'),
  (77, 594.740, 614.784, 'Then someone came to me right away, one of those people who said to me, ''Listen, you have to go to that man there from the police with your child and your wife, because they''ll let your wife and child out.'''),
  (78, 619.080, 626.759, 'My wife, who wanted... first situation, ''No, I''m leaving. Go to that family and tell them I''m here.'''),
  (79, 627.799, 631.480, 'So I go there. My wife was allowed out.'),
  (80, 633.575, 638.215, 'I went there. I went there. I don''t know what it is, what the street was called.'),
  (81, 640.215, 656.320, 'And around 7 o''clock, Saly Noah came. We saw from a distance that we had to stay calm.'),
  (82, 657.120, 666.475, 'Meanwhile, at that big long table, there were all military, I don''t know.'),
  (83, 668.465, 672.993, 'And there in front of me was [Klaus] Barbie, sitting there.'),
  (84, 673.454, 682.325, 'And they asked everyone how much money you had, papers, yes, I don''t know, all kinds of things.'),
  (85, 684.230, 695.795, 'And then people were then divided. ''Camp'' in French, also ''camp de travail.'''),
  (86, 698.915, 702.779, 'Some were to go here and others could there.'),
  (87, 703.019, 709.339, 'But meanwhile we were a group of 10 or 12.'),
  (88, 710.683, 714.026, 'We had papers.'),
  (89, 715.804, 722.284, 'Now at 8, 8 o''clock or so, somehow got out.'),
  (90, 724.365, 727.164, 'Came out. That was the first one.'),
  (91, 729.269, 738.549, 'Later we heard that camp went directly to Drancy, went to Auschwitz.'),
  (92, 738.789, 741.429, 'And that camp de travail was still in France.'),
  (93, 744.495, 746.495, 'It was the first raid.'),
  (94, 749.615, 750.815, 'What should you do?'),
  (95, 752.095, 753.535, 'I don''t know what you should do.'),
  (96, 754.735, 761.600, 'So I tried... yes, the police, what they did ask, that''s true.'),
  (97, 761.600, 768.559, 'I had to show proof that I was leaving France.'),
  (98, 769.834, 779.834, 'He asked about family. I told him I have a brother in Chile who should send a telegram, that he should send a visa.'),
  (99, 781.674, 783.274, 'I still have the answer with me.'),
  (100, 784.679, 788.840, 'He writes, ''I will send a visa as soon as possible.'''),
  (101, 789.160, 803.855, 'Because even if you had that, you had to have a visa de sortie and a transit visa.'),
  (102, 804.174, 808.495, 'To be able to get to Spain, and then continue.'),
  (103, 808.894, 815.129, 'And then I went to the consulate of Spain and to the French.'),
  (104, 815.209, 817.209, '''Yes, if you don''t have that, you can''t do it.'''),
  (105, 817.209, 818.169, 'But well, it all didn''t work.'),
  (106, 818.169, 826.490, 'After another week, that was September.'),
  (107, 828.824, 830.584, 'It was the second raid.'),
  (108, 832.345, 833.784, 'Still 1942.'),
  (109, 834.105, 835.464, 'Everything in 1942.'),
  (110, 835.784, 837.625, 'I''m in Lyon then.'),
  (111, 847.090, 856.129, 'In the second raid we weren''t arrested.'),
  (112, 856.514, 861.154, 'My niece and my nephew and their son.'),
  (113, 861.715, 869.554, 'They were with us, unfortunately they went downstairs, then they found them, arrested them.'),
  (114, 872.710, 890.274, 'Then in the morning, it was also at night, and in the morning suddenly, at 7 o''clock I think, someone knocked on the door. It was my niece with a police officer or gendarme, I don''t know exactly.'),
  (115, 891.875, 911.100, 'She says, ''I want to take some things with me, and give me some French money.'' She gave me some valuable things of hers.'),
  (116, 911.820, 919.644, 'I said, ''Listen, maybe talk to your husband. We''ll give you some money. They''ll leave you here.'''),
  (117, 921.085, 924.524, '''No, I won''t do that because I''m going back to my husband with the child.'''),
  (118, 926.539, 940.059, 'And she gave me the name of a police doctor who got them out the first time. ''Maybe you can try to do something.'''),
  (119, 941.615, 942.894, 'I said, ''I''ll try.'''),
  (120, 943.215, 946.654, 'That was on Friday, from Thursday to Friday.'),
  (121, 949.774, 960.549, 'I had no connections, but I had acquaintances from Holland who spoke good French. ''Listen, you''re coming with me to that doctor there.'''),
  (122, 965.190, 966.789, 'The Franc wasn''t worth much anymore.'),
  (123, 971.955, 976.595, '''Listen, you got that Kern family out then.'''),
  (124, 977.075, 978.274, '''You''ll see, they have to.'''),
  (125, 982.009, 988.250, 'I said, ''Do you have the money? I don''t know what you need. See what you can do.'''),
  (126, 989.049, 991.289, '''I need to know where they are.'''),
  (127, 992.730, 994.009, '''I don''t know that either.'''),
  (128, 995.764, 997.365, '''You have to find out first.'''),
  (129, 999.365, 1017.350, 'On Saturday there was a camp outside Lyon. It was a barracks of Indo-Chinese.'),
  (130, 1017.429, 1022.309, 'The French still had those Chinese troops.'),
  (131, 1023.924, 1025.444, 'Very large.'),
  (132, 1027.365, 1038.099, 'We came there by car with a package, with the name Kern written on it.'),
  (133, 1040.099, 1043.859, 'Went to the entrance with soldiers in front.'),
  (134, 1043.940, 1048.740, 'She said, ''Listen, hundreds of people inside. I don''t know how many.'''),
  (135, 1050.815, 1059.454, '''I''ll give this. Maybe if those people are there, then I''ll know the people are there.'''),
  (136, 1059.775, 1060.894, 'He took it.'),
  (137, 1067.450, 1078.169, 'Standing outside on the other side of the sidewalk were young French Jewish young men whose parents were inside.'),
  (138, 1080.194, 1083.315, 'Then I went off and screamed at those soldiers.'),
  (139, 1083.315, 1092.355, 'You saw a few times you saw big cars driving in.'),
  (140, 1092.355, 1098.009, 'That was a big thing, of course, with people.'),
  (141, 1101.289, 1103.210, 'We went back. There was nothing to be done.'),
  (142, 1103.210, 1107.690, 'The next day we heard that they were there again.'),
  (143, 1107.690, 1131.440, 'So the next day, that was on Sunday, that roundup... the price of it all... she perished in the struggle, and the other one, my nephew with that boy, they survived until the end. They met someone.'),
  (144, 1131.519, 1147.254, 'They worked in Buchenwald, and in Buchenwald they had to march, had to go back. And that father and that son were on that road.'),
  (145, 1147.414, 1152.855, 'That father was shot dead, and that son who lives, he still lives in Vienna.'),
  (146, 1155.619, 1162.339, 'We were young, so we had to see about getting away, because it wasn''t working.'),
  (147, 1162.659, 1170.339, 'So I had to... but you can''t go to the police or to Spain or to Switzerland.'),
  (148, 1170.845, 1174.204, 'Spain was not possible to go over the mountains.'),
  (149, 1175.325, 1186.650, 'Switzerland too, but well, you had to have a travel permit to be able to ride the train. That was from Lyon to Annemasse.'),
  (150, 1187.049, 1191.210, 'That''s the place opposite Geneva, on the French side.'),
  (151, 1191.850, 1196.330, 'Then you had to have a permit. You had to get that from the police.'),
  (152, 1197.325, 1208.525, 'I went to the police with a form, with a pack of cigarettes, and the next... well, in the afternoon I believe, I was allowed to pick it up.'),
  (153, 1209.244, 1222.980, 'It was written down with names and everything, and I already told you, ''Confession: Protestant.'' So I could... they did check it later, of course.'),
  (154, 1224.580, 1231.825, 'And then the first day I went alone with a family. I thought, ''First let me see where I can find something.'''),
  (155, 1232.384, 1240.065, 'It was a mistake, but well, I went back to Lyon, picked up my wife and child. We went back to Annemasse.'),
  (156, 1243.570, 1248.369, 'It didn''t work. That road was broken again. Couldn''t cross.'),
  (157, 1254.065, 1263.025, 'That Annemasse, that was a border town. Just look for where, how you can get across.'),
  (158, 1265.069, 1273.069, 'I knocked on various families who lived there, Jewish families. They were all terrified that I came to the door.'),
  (159, 1274.349, 1282.085, 'We found someone who said, ''I know someone who can bring you across.'''),
  (160, 1282.565, 1288.005, 'It cost something like 200 francs, I believe.'),
  (161, 1294.440, 1313.634, 'We went with another family. They had a girl who was 4, 5 years old. And that man arranged, ''You have to take that bus, then you have to get off there.'''),
  (162, 1313.954, 1317.315, '''And when you get off there, I''ll be standing there, and then we''ll continue.'''),
  (163, 1320.400, 1324.559, 'We got on a bus the next day.'),
  (164, 1329.119, 1331.759, 'We got off wrong, early.'),
  (165, 1332.320, 1336.604, 'Those other people were people who had lived in France.'),
  (166, 1338.284, 1341.084, 'Got off. We saw no one on the road.'),
  (167, 1342.125, 1345.244, 'We waited a while. Then that man came with his bicycle.'),
  (168, 1345.244, 1347.085, 'He says, ''Why? Where are you?'''),
  (169, 1347.085, 1348.605, '''Well, we were supposed to get off.'''),
  (170, 1352.329, 1362.389, 'We walked to the border. The Ggendarme said ''papers''.'),
  (171, 1362.581, 1366.038, 'I gave him papers.'),
  (172, 1367.734, 1374.295, '''That''s not right. Just go away.'''),
  (173, 1376.055, 1377.975, 'And I let him lead.'),
  (174, 1380.660, 1392.180, 'And that man, the smuggler... the place was called Route Nationale. This side was Switzerland and this side was France.'),
  (175, 1392.340, 1395.299, 'There it went a bit uphill.'),
  (176, 1396.045, 1401.005, 'And that man went up the mountain with us, so not that side.'),
  (177, 1401.085, 1404.044, 'Because the gendarmes were there.'),
  (178, 1407.724, 1448.440, 'We walked maybe 10 minutes. In any case, a car came. We all had to get in, and it brought us to a post, a gendarmerie post.'),
  (179, 1450.825, 1461.305, 'And that driver of that car, the gendarme, not the gendarmerie, was an angel.'),
  (180, 1464.660, 1470.419, 'He drove and said, ''I have to pay that man for that ride.'''),
  (181, 1471.859, 1485.134, 'It was already dark, so I gave that man money, because he stopped waving back with that money.'),
  (182, 1487.454, 1488.335, 'He left.'),
  (183, 1491.049, 1493.849, 'Then we came to the gendarmerie post.'),
  (184, 1495.609, 1501.210, 'My wife with the child were allowed to sleep in their house.'),
  (185, 1501.930, 1507.144, 'We were interrogated every half hour or quarter hour.'),
  (186, 1507.785, 1510.585, '''You wanted to go illegally over the border.'''),
  (187, 1512.664, 1514.904, '''You''re Jews.'' ''We''re not Jews''.'),
  (188, 1516.184, 1517.545, 'I had my Talit and Tfilin with me.'),
  (189, 1524.230, 1525.910, 'That went on all night.'),
  (190, 1525.910, 1529.509, 'Terrible things happened.'),
  (191, 1529.589, 1532.309, 'A woman came in with 2 daughters.'),
  (192, 1534.715, 1535.674, 'Screaming.'),
  (193, 1537.515, 1548.089, 'And 2 boys, 2 French boys, who had that woman with those 2 daughters, were supposed to bring them over the border.'),
  (194, 1548.650, 1551.130, 'They probably made a mistake. I don''t know.'),
  (195, 1551.130, 1553.049, 'In any case, they were caught again.'),
  (196, 1553.930, 1559.850, 'And that gendarme asked the boys how much money they got from that woman.'),
  (197, 1564.585, 1567.144, 'They also beat up those 2 heads.'),
  (198, 1568.984, 1578.130, 'So they''d tell... the woman, those 2 daughters, the next day they came to Rivesaltes.'),
  (199, 1578.130, 1579.650, 'Have you heard of Rivesaltes?'),
  (200, 1580.049, 1583.170, 'Just like the transit camp.'),
  (201, 1585.330, 1587.330, 'They came from there. They had to go back.'),
  (202, 1591.724, 1617.400, 'The next day, it was in the morning, and I tried... and my wife got a little cart from the house there, so they could put the child in it. And we saw the street there. You could see the border, the barrier. And you saw the Swiss soldiers. Here you saw the French soldiers.'),
  (203, 1617.400, 1620.505, 'It was a little bit open. There was a border.'),
  (204, 1624.721, 1629.840, 'I said to my wife.'),
  (205, 1629.840, 1638.926, 'She has to walk with her pram as close as she could to the border, so she could try to cross with the child.'),
  (206, 1645.259, 1654.834, 'I heard one of them. there was no road. I could speak German. He was from Alsace.'),
  (207, 1655.315, 1657.315, 'The matter was already over.'),
  (208, 1661.309, 1663.950, '''You see, ma''am, the child is sick.'''),
  (209, 1665.150, 1671.230, '''Maybe it''s possible that the Swiss Red Cross can take the womean and child.'''),
  (210, 1675.565, 1680.444, '''I''ll ask about it.'''),
  (211, 1680.525, 1684.444, 'And that commander got a scolding there.'),
  (212, 1686.204, 1690.799, '''You have to lock that man up.'''),
  (213, 1691.119, 1700.240, 'I come outside. ''You come here, and if I do anything more, I''ll be handcuffed.'''),
  (214, 1703.142, 1711.776, 'They then took everything we had and all our money from us, I still have the receipt of that.'),
  (215, 1716.289, 1720.929, 'Then we were allowed to sit in a little café and wait.'),
  (216, 1721.009, 1734.535, 'That same gendarme who stopped us the very first time came to pick us up at 3:30. We had to go back to Lyon.'),
  (217, 1734.535, 1740.454, 'On the note it said we had to report to the police at the commissariat.'),
  (218, 1743.575, 1750.990, 'He came to us, picked us up, and we started walking on the Route Nationale.'),
  (219, 1754.829, 1758.910, 'I saw that here, this side must be Switzerland.'),
  (220, 1761.755, 1775.680, 'We walked a bit. I saw a house standing on the right side, and then stairs going up. Those stairs were still on the French side, and after the stairs was barbed wire.'),
  (221, 1778.799, 1785.039, 'But one piece was pushed away, a little bit open, a little bit open.'),
  (222, 1785.680, 1786.720, 'I saw that.'),
  (223, 1787.039, 1793.815, 'I was carrying a child, with the bag, and the other couple was also there.'),
  (224, 1794.215, 1797.255, 'And I said to myself, ''I''m going through here.'''),
  (225, 1799.734, 1804.375, 'Because that gendarme who was just with us, he was walking in front of us.')) v(seq, s, e, x)
where t.slug = 'tape-3';

-- Tape 4: captions (4).sbv, 184 cues
insert into entities (type, slug, name, attrs) values ('tape', 'tape-4', 'Tape 4', '{"number": 4, "youtube_id": null, "duration_sec": 1425.4}') on conflict (slug) do nothing;
delete from cues where lang = 'en' and tape_id = (select id from entities where slug = 'tape-4');
insert into cues (tape_id, lang, seq, start_sec, end_sec, text)
select t.id, 'en', v.seq, v.s, v.e, v.x from entities t, (values
  (1, 0.640, 3.520, 'The barbed wire, toward the Swiss side.'),
  (2, 3.520, 5.520, 'The Swiss side and there we were met.'),
  (3, 6.240, 12.320, 'By a Swiss customs officer.'),
  (4, 14.085, 18.240, 'He brought us to the other side of the border.'),
  (5, 19.925, 29.200, 'to a Swiss gendarmerie post, or something like that.'),
  (6, 30.440, 33.960, 'That was around four o''clock, quarter past four.'),
  (7, 35.000, 42.200, 'But it took until eight o''clock, half past eight maybe. I don''t know exactly.'),
  (8, 42.520, 46.900, 'before they received permission to let us into the country.'),
  (9, 46.900, 49.930, 'because actually they would have sent us back.'),
  (10, 50.170, 53.300, 'we were lucky that we had the child with us.'),
  (11, 53.770, 57.930, 'otherwise they would have sent us back, just like they sent back thousands of others.'),
  (12, 60.820, 65.620, 'This was September, the beginning of September.'),
  (13, 66.660, 70.180, 'three days before Rosh Hashanah, if I am not mistaken.'),
  (14, 75.500, 87.920, 'We were taken by car to a reception camp, Warembé, near Geneva.'),
  (15, 90.258, 97.028, 'We slept in a small hall on straw. I don''t know exactly what it was.'),
  (16, 98.040, 105.000, 'My child was sick, so the next morning I went straight to the commander, or whoever was in charge.'),
  (17, 105.480, 110.520, 'I said: listen, my child is sick, we need, we need medical help.'),
  (18, 113.220, 124.740, 'We were taken by car to Geneva, to a small hotel near the station.'),
  (19, 132.880, 142.830, 'We went straight to a children''s home to ask if they would take our child in for a while.'),
  (20, 143.950, 152.510, 'They said: we would be glad to do that, but you must first take the child to the hospital to be examined.'),
  (21, 153.768, 160.120, 'He stayed there for two months, with serious infections of all kinds.'),
  (22, 165.810, 167.320, 'And we were allowed to stay in the little hotel.'),
  (23, 169.567, 172.997, 'That was about ten, twelve days.'),
  (24, 173.834, 175.930, 'During Yom Kippur we were still there.'),
  (25, 176.490, 180.288, 'We came home in the evening to the little hotel.'),
  (26, 183.220, 193.310, 'A Family had already told us since you are here, you will be with us for Sukkoth. They were Swiss Jews.'),
  (27, 196.890, 209.530, 'I helped set up a kitchen there for the refugees to eat. I helped with the tables and everything.'),
  (28, 211.600, 217.830, 'I came home and there was a letter from the police. We had to be at the station at eight in the morning.'),
  (29, 218.641, 226.271, 'Cornavin station.'),
  (30, 227.170, 233.517, 'I went to the police and said, listen that is not possible, our child is in the hospital.'),
  (31, 233.922, 237.976, '"you still have to be at the station in the morning."'),
  (32, 238.680, 249.797, 'So we went. At the station there were 120 people waiting.'),
  (33, 251.690, 257.200, 'We were ordered into a train.'),
  (34, 258.810, 261.770, 'But first I must tell you something about what happened in Geneva.'),
  (35, 266.620, 272.290, 'Suddenly, while we were at the hotel, we received a summons to appear before the police.'),
  (36, 275.110, 280.977, 'I went there with my wife, but I had to go in alone.'),
  (37, 281.430, 286.902, 'And then he said you are being sent back to France.'),
  (38, 288.400, 293.019, '“What is the reason?” “You lied".'),
  (39, 294.000, 298.677, '“You have deceived us. You are not a Dutchman”.'),
  (40, 302.680, 306.653, 'I said, but don''t you have the documents?'),
  (41, 308.200, 313.939, '“We called the consulate in Geneva”'),
  (42, 315.560, 318.227, '“and they said that Stemmer is not a Dutch citizen”.'),
  (43, 325.070, 327.684, '“So you have lied. You are going back to France”.'),
  (44, 328.680, 335.930, 'I started shouting. My wife heard something was wrong and came in.'),
  (45, 336.170, 339.850, '"What is going on?" "Listen, they are sending us back."'),
  (46, 354.020, 355.940, 'It all came to a good end. That was during those few days in Geneva.'),
  (47, 357.996, 365.770, 'We were put on the train. The train was locked by soldiers with bayonets.'),
  (48, 367.010, 370.290, '120 people locked in one carriage.'),
  (49, 371.480, 376.190, 'The train rode through Switzerland.'),
  (50, 377.000, 384.680, 'At every transfer point they uncoupled our carriage and connected it to another train.'),
  (51, 386.370, 391.170, 'So we did not have to get out. Otherwise we might have run for it, I suppose.'),
  (52, 391.650, 395.730, 'We rode around until late afternoon.'),
  (53, 397.890, 400.690, 'We arrived in a small place called Eriswil.'),
  (54, 401.880, 405.384, 'A small place. The train went no further. That was the end of the line.'),
  (55, 405.837, 409.102, 'We ended up in an old school building.'),
  (56, 413.250, 418.670, 'We slept there too on straw with blankets. Our child was still in Geneva.'),
  (57, 422.430, 437.190, 'My wife and I were there together. After about two months our child was brought and we fetched him.'),
  (58, 440.280, 447.400, 'I still have all the letters from that hospital in Geneva.'),
  (59, 450.440, 457.770, 'Then the camp commander received word that my wife and child had to leave.'),
  (60, 458.500, 467.978, 'They went to an internment home called Bienenberg, near Liestal, not far from Basel.'),
  (61, 474.210, 486.467, 'When they arrived it turned out to be an old hotel, with almost no heating. They were still setting it up for the refugees.'),
  (62, 487.492, 492.739, 'Naturally the child fell ill again right away.'),
  (63, 492.824, 498.594, 'In those four years, until November 1945, he was in five or six hospitals.'),
  (64, 499.448, 501.187, 'Every time, always sick again.'),
  (65, 501.650, 508.835, 'In any case, in ''43, March or April, I don''t know exactly.'),
  (66, 509.940, 516.320, 'I was transferred to Adliswil. 1943'),
  (67, 517.220, 520.500, 'From September I was there the whole time, in Adliswil.'),
  (68, 525.740, 528.503, 'Adliswil is a small town near Zurich.'),
  (69, 531.660, 537.260, 'I was there for a while. I was not, no, I was not yet allowed to visit my wife.'),
  (70, 537.820, 547.650, 'That only came later, in the work camp. In the work camp, in ''43. I still have the documents.'),
  (71, 550.740, 555.720, 'I have my identity card from the Dutch consulate in Bern, and it is also recorded in my Swiss pass.'),
  (72, 555.720, 559.540, 'And I also have my Swiss pass it''s all recorded there.'),
  (73, 559.540, 562.540, 'It is all written there. Somewhere end of ''43.'),
  (74, 563.580, 569.778, 'No, no, no. It must have been March or April.'),
  (75, 570.870, 577.639, 'From Adliswil I went to the work camp Le Chaluet.'),
  (76, 578.240, 582.160, 'Near Moutier, in the Berner Oberland, as we called it.'),
  (77, 583.920, 586.886, 'You have seen the photo. That is Le Chaluet.'),
  (78, 588.000, 591.430, 'I was there for a long time. I fell ill. I could not walk anymore.'),
  (79, 592.390, 597.840, 'I got muscle rheumatism. There was no toilet there.'),
  (80, 598.320, 603.120, 'Just a barrack that served as a toilet. You had to go there in the middle of the night.'),
  (81, 604.000, 612.208, 'It was the worst area in Switzerland. Terrible.'),
  (82, 613.290, 619.690, 'The nearest village was five or six kilometers away.'),
  (83, 622.290, 629.650, 'Every six weeks you got three days leave to go and visit your wife.'),
  (84, 631.410, 642.202, 'You earned one franc fifty a day, of which seventy five cents were kept back for later.'),
  (85, 645.540, 652.650, 'After that I was moved to another work camp called Raron.'),
  (86, 653.450, 657.760, 'Near Brig, all the way south, close to the Italian border.'),
  (87, 661.030, 671.990, 'Because I was ill, I got leave twice to go to Baden near Zurich, to take the baths.'),
  (88, 673.190, 677.920, 'They tried everything, injections and so on. I could not walk anymore.'),
  (89, 681.120, 685.600, 'Then I came to the internment camp Morgins. Morgin that is in.'),
  (90, 690.638, 696.064, 'In the Valais, right on the French border.'),
  (91, 702.135, 706.970, 'Only in 1944 did I manage to bring my wife and child there too.'),
  (92, 707.945, 716.406, 'There were three hotels: Grand Hotel, Victoria and Le Foret.'),
  (93, 717.730, 722.935, 'We were housed there, but the child had to go somewhere else.'),
  (94, 722.935, 727.924, 'That did not work at all. He stopped eating. It was terrible.'),
  (95, 730.000, 739.613, 'He was in hospital again. Lausanne, Zurich, Baden, Geneva.'),
  (96, 742.550, 747.950, 'And so it went on, until the liberation came.'),
  (97, 751.550, 757.087, 'We still could not go home. The liberation was May 1945.'),
  (98, 758.990, 766.470, 'There too we were interned. We listened to the English radio.'),
  (99, 771.497, 776.707, 'I was not allowed to return to the Netherlands, because I was not a Dutch citizen.'),
  (100, 784.980, 791.660, 'To tell it briefly, we arrived on my birthday, November 7th, 1945.'),
  (101, 793.453, 802.359, 'By train from Basel, through France, back to Holland, to Eindhoven.'),
  (102, 802.620, 806.470, 'In the factory hall of Philips we slept there for a night. We registered.'),
  (103, 808.470, 815.490, 'I still have those papers. Displaced person, I don''t know what.'),
  (104, 815.490, 817.190, 'I still have all those documents.'),
  (105, 820.179, 828.328, 'We had no house. In Eindhoven at that time was the Jewish Brigade.'),
  (106, 833.540, 845.940, 'We had a good acquaintance who had a non-Jewish wife. He was in Westerbork but got out because of the mixed marriage.'),
  (107, 847.020, 855.580, 'He lived in Amsterdam on the Rijnstraat and wrote to us right after the war, when you arrive, come to us.'),
  (108, 861.350, 870.396, 'Meanwhile we also had a second child. He was born May 29th, 1945.'),
  (109, 873.020, 884.380, 'In Switzerland, in Montreux. We were up in the hills above Montreux.'),
  (110, 887.540, 889.540, 'so she had to go to a hospital.'),
  (111, 891.300, 892.724, 'So, you went to Amsterdam?'),
  (112, 893.620, 897.240, 'No, my wife went to Amsterdam with the two children. I went to The Hague.'),
  (113, 897.240, 899.940, 'And I went to The Hague to see what was going on.'),
  (114, 903.810, 908.040, 'The city hall was gone. It was now in the Goudenregenstraat.'),
  (115, 908.770, 913.410, 'I said, I used to live here, and I was forced to leave.'),
  (116, 916.140, 929.580, 'Before we fled I had dealt with the man who became the owner. My house had been sold during the war.'),
  (117, 933.340, 939.159, 'I said, "but I used to live here, and I had my business here."'),
  (118, 940.206, 944.580, 'They said, you cannot go back there. You will just have to find somewhere else to live.'),
  (119, 946.420, 951.700, 'There was nothing. I met some people and slept where I could.'),
  (120, 954.360, 959.550, 'On Prinsmauritslaan there was a house that had been managed by the Jewish community.'),
  (121, 960.670, 965.171, 'I slept there. Then I got a room in the city, on the Hoefkade.'),
  (122, 968.260, 975.060, 'And then there was the house of my parents-in-law, who had been taken away in September 1942.'),
  (123, 975.780, 980.630, 'They were taken by that Judenfischer, Franz Fischer, that murderer.'),
  (124, 982.790, 991.430, 'Because my sister-in-law was sitting on a bench with the twins, two girls of three years old.'),
  (125, 992.390, 1000.234, 'In the Belgisch Park, you know it. Fischer walked past and arrested them.'),
  (126, 1000.450, 1008.290, 'In any case, the whole family, my parents-in-law, their three daughters and the twins,'),
  (127, 1009.090, 1011.445, 'were brought to the Windekind. You know it too. They stayed there one night.'),
  (128, 1011.445, 1015.065, 'You''ll know it. They stayed there for one night.'),
  (129, 1015.070, 1019.540, 'I have proof of that. Someone who visited them there told me.'),
  (130, 1020.900, 1026.920, 'They were classified as a punishment case. Within ten days they were dead in Auschwitz.'),
  (131, 1029.240, 1033.080, 'That was my wife''s story. That is why I do not want to say more about it.'),
  (132, 1033.960, 1041.207, 'I came back. Not long after I received a notice from the Ministry,'),
  (133, 1043.724, 1051.490, 'Military Affairs or something, stating that I owed a certain amount for the return journey from Basel to Eindhoven.'),
  (134, 1053.430, 1053.930, 'Very kind indeed.'),
  (135, 1057.170, 1057.270, 'I went there and said: Listen, I have nothing. I don''t even have a place to live.'),
  (136, 1057.270, 1062.710, 'I went there and said, "listen, I have nothing. I don''t even have a place to live."'),
  (137, 1063.190, 1063.290, 'They said, if you do not pay, you will be undesirable and sent back to Switzerland.'),
  (138, 1063.290, 1068.975, 'They said, If you don''t pay, you''ll be an undesirable person and sent back to Switzerland.'),
  (139, 1074.730, 1080.351, 'I said, I did not say I do not want to pay. I said I cannot pay right now.'),
  (140, 1083.300, 1089.700, 'So we drew up a contract: I would pay one guilder a month.'),
  (141, 1093.510, 1096.080, 'A few weeks later I got a letter. They had made a mistake.'),
  (142, 1096.630, 1102.550, 'It was not, it was not that much, it was not 500 guilders. It was 500 Swiss francs.'),
  (143, 1102.950, 1105.740, 'And at the time the franc was worth less than the guilder, so I owed less.'),
  (144, 1106.760, 1106.860, 'Still, I paid the full amount a few months later.'),
  (145, 1106.860, 1110.300, 'Still, I paid the full amount a few months later.'),
  (146, 1114.540, 1116.695, 'Well, I think I have covered it more or less.'),
  (147, 1121.160, 1125.143, 'My father, my father and my sister.'),
  (148, 1126.168, 1136.247, 'My sister, in April, in April, with four children, was taken to Sobibor.'),
  (149, 1138.910, 1147.740, 'My father was on the last transport. That was August, August 1943.'),
  (150, 1147.740, 1150.610, 'No, 43.'),
  (151, 1150.610, 1155.204, 'No no. Sobibor was closed.'),
  (152, 1158.020, 1164.580, 'Yes, yes. Not to Sobibor, because Sobibor was shut down by then.'),
  (153, 1166.340, 1173.210, 'That was the last transport. I believe that is what happened to my father.'),
  (154, 1175.300, 1181.450, 'I don''t know if you know it, the 17th of Tammuz, a Jewish fast day.'),
  (155, 1182.020, 1191.700, 'On that day all the chief rabbis of The Hague, all the other rabbis, were deported to Sobibor.'),
  (156, 1192.580, 1195.620, 'You can read it all in that book about Sobibor.'),
  (157, 1200.990, 1202.110, 'Did you then move into that house?'),
  (158, 1202.990, 1210.510, 'No, I had to go and live in that house, but that, well, that did not just happen.'),
  (159, 1210.980, 1216.340, 'After the war a property management institute had been set up.'),
  (160, 1217.620, 1221.300, 'I was told to go there, so I went.'),
  (161, 1222.100, 1227.510, '"Oh, is that you? We have already appointed an administrator for you. We assumed you were not coming back either".'),
  (162, 1229.009, 1233.089, 'He gave me the name. The administrator was a teacher in Rijswijk.'),
  (163, 1234.070, 1241.480, 'We contacted him and he was very glad that at least someone came back and he could hand it over.'),
  (164, 1243.960, 1248.680, 'He had been managing both my parents-in-law''s property and mine.'),
  (165, 1249.160, 1254.770, 'But the house was uninhabitable. There was no glass in the windows.'),
  (166, 1255.090, 1261.960, 'That man arranged it. He was head of a school, I think. He got glass,'),
  (167, 1263.570, 1268.440, 'from the horticultural trade. The glass was installed, and the house was made a little livable.'),
  (168, 1268.440, 1273.100, 'The house was made a little livable, and we got some furniture.'),
  (169, 1273.100, 1275.240, 'We got some furniture. At that management institute you could get furniture. That is how we started.'),
  (170, 1275.240, 1280.220, 'There was that management institute where you could get furniture. That''s how we started.'),
  (171, 1285.963, 1289.742, 'On the right is my father David Loeb Stemmer'),
  (172, 1292.133, 1296.098, 'On the left side, you can see my mother, Augusta Stemmer-Better.'),
  (173, 1298.745, 1305.357, 'My mother was about thirty years old when this was taken.'),
  (174, 1312.146, 1324.913, 'Look. The photo of my father was taken here in Holland, around 1935, 1936.'),
  (175, 1331.617, 1341.085, 'Here you can see my brother, second from the left.'),
  (176, 1341.555, 1349.618, 'All the way on the right is my sister-in-law. They went to Palestine in 1935.'),
  (177, 1349.618, 1352.436, 'And in front of him is his son.'),
  (178, 1356.668, 1365.170, 'I am standing all the way on the left. Next to me is a small boy, their son.'),
  (179, 1368.885, 1374.958, 'He is Professor Shamir from Jerusalem. And behind him stands my father.'),
  (180, 1376.025, 1381.526, 'And my sister and my brother-in-law.'),
  (181, 1394.464, 1400.331, 'Yes, my mother is here, and my father is carrying the youngest on his arm.'),
  (182, 1403.277, 1406.984, 'Yes, that is the professor in Jerusalem.'),
  (183, 1409.119, 1414.310, 'The other ones now also live in Jerusalem.'),
  (184, 1414.310, 1420.404, 'No, not all of them, some perished in Sobibor. The youngest one is my sister’s.')) v(seq, s, e, x)
where t.slug = 'tape-4';
